# Build context allow-list for e2e-cross-sdk/Dockerfile.
#
# ── The filename matters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This was `.dockerignore` sitting next to the Dockerfile, which Docker never
# reads: it resolves `.dockerignore` relative to the CONTEXT ROOT, and
# docker-compose.yml sets `context: ../..` — the monorepo root one level above
# this repo. So none of the rules had ever applied. `docker buildx build --check`
# confirmed it: `load .dockerignore` reported `transferring context: 2B`, an
# empty ignore list. BuildKit does honour `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` next
# to the Dockerfile, which is what this name is for.
#
# ── Deny-all + allow-list, not a blacklist ───────────────────────────────────
# The context is a whole multi-repo dev directory, so a blacklist has to name
# every artifact dir, sibling checkout and stray download that ever appears
# there — and the previous one had already fallen behind. The Dockerfile has a
# small, fixed set of COPY sources, so inverting it bounds the context
# permanently: anything not listed below is excluded, and a COPY of an
# unlisted path fails loudly at build time instead of being silently included.
#
# Keep this in sync with the COPYs in the Dockerfile. Every `!` entry below
# corresponds to one.
#
# ── Scope of the win ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Modest, and worth stating honestly: BuildKit derives its context
# include-patterns from the COPY sources, so paths no COPY references are not
# transferred whether they are ignored or not. Measured on a synthetic 300 MB
# context, an unreferenced directory added nothing to the transfer. What this
# saves is the metadata walk over a large dev tree (locally, where sibling
# repos and a multi-GB cognee-rust/target/ live), plus the guarantee that a
# future COPY cannot accidentally start pulling in an artifact dir.

# Exclude everything by default.
*

# ── Rust side (cognee-rust/) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Release binaries built on the runner (see .github/workflows/http-parity.yml)
# or by hand for a local build. NOT excluded — the harness stage COPYs these.
!cognee-rust/dist/cognee-cli
!cognee-rust/dist/cognee-http-server
!cognee-rust/dist/cognee-telemetry-emit

# Harness: pytest suite, entrypoint scripts, fixtures.
!cognee-rust/e2e-cross-sdk/harness
!cognee-rust/e2e-cross-sdk/bin
!cognee-rust/e2e-cross-sdk/test_data

# ── Python side (cognee/, the sibling repo) ──────────────────────────────────
# uv needs the manifest, the lockfile and README (referenced by pyproject).
!cognee/pyproject.toml
!cognee/uv.lock
!cognee/README.md
# The packages cognee's wheel declares. At the pinned SHA that is
#   [tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel] packages = ["cognee", "cognee_db_workers", "kuzu"]
# `distributed` is kept for older pins that packaged it instead.
# This file is an allowlist (`*` above, `!` re-includes), so a package missing
# here is absent from the build context entirely and its COPY fails.
!cognee/cognee
!cognee/cognee_db_workers
!cognee/distributed

# ── Re-exclusions inside the allowed trees ───────────────────────────────────
# `!` above re-includes whole directories, so noise inside them comes back.
# Cargo/Python artifacts cannot appear under the allowed paths today, but
# __pycache__ readily does — the harness and the cognee package are both run
# in-place during local development.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
**/.pytest_cache
**/.DS_Store
